## Summary of the changes
- [X] Support for virtualizing collections present in memory.
- [X] Support for virtualizing collections from an asynchronous data source.
- [x] Added functional and E2E tests

## Usage

Virtualizing an in-memory list:

```
<Virtualize Items="@fixedItems" ItemSize="itemSize">
    <div style="height: @(itemSize)px;">Item @context</div>
</Virtualize>

@code {
    float itemSize = 100;
    ICollection<int> fixedItems = Enumerable.Range(0, 1000).ToList();
}
```

Virtualizing an asynchronous item source:

```
<Virtualize ItemsProvider="GetItemsAsync" ItemSize="itemSize">
    <Item>
        <div style="height: @(itemSize)px; background-color: green;">Item @context</div>
    </Item>
    <Placeholder>
        <div style="height: @(itemSize)px; background-color: red;">Loading item @context.Index...</div>
    </Placeholder>
</Virtualize>

@code {
    float itemSize = 100;
    int totalItemCount = 200;

    async ValueTask<ItemsProviderResult<int>> GetItemsAsync(ItemsProviderRequest request)
    {
        await Task.Delay(500, request.CancellationToken);

        return new ItemsProviderResult<int>(Enumerable.Range(request.StartIndex, request.Count), totalItemCount);
    }
}
```

If someone wanted to cache asynchronously-fetched items in memory so `<Virtualize>` wouldn't fetch the same item twice, they could cache items after they're fetched in the `ItemsProvider`, returning any cached items synchronously.

## Potential future features
* Defining a custom container margin
* Allowing users to leave `ItemsSize` unspecified
* Horizontal scrolling

